diff --git a/debian/patches/wsh/0120-target-sparc-keep-SuperSPARC-instruction-fetches-in-.patch b/debian/patches/wsh/0120-target-sparc-keep-SuperSPARC-instruction-fetches-in-.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a4884fb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/wsh/0120-target-sparc-keep-SuperSPARC-instruction-fetches-in-.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] target/sparc: keep SuperSPARC instruction fetches in the PROM in boot mode with the MMU on
+
+Sun's SPARCserver 600MP boot PROM stops QEMU within seconds of power-on,
+before it prints anything: "Trap 0x01 (Instruction Access Fault) while
+interrupts disabled, Error state". Its MMU register test, run in boot mode
+from the PROM with traps disabled, walks a one through the writable bits of
+the MMU control register, the enable bit (bit 0) among them. QEMU translates
+every instruction fetch once the MMU is enabled; with no page tables set up
+yet, the next fetch faults, and so does the fetch of the trap vector.
+
+On the SuperSPARC (Viking) the boot mode bit (bit 13 of the MMU control
+register) sends every instruction fetch to the boot PROM, at
+0xf.f000_0000 plus the low 28 bits of the address, whether the MMU is
+enabled or not. Data accesses are not affected by it and are translated
+when the MMU is on (Linux's arch/sparc/include/asm/viking.h documents the
+bit this way). QEMU applied boot mode only while the MMU was disabled.
+
+Add the CPU feature "boot-mode-ifetch", set on the TI-SuperSparc-* models.
+With it, sparc_cpu_mmu_index() returns MMU_PHYS_IDX for an instruction
+fetch while boot mode is set, and get_physical_address() maps the fetch to
+the PROM as it already does with the MMU off. Data accesses keep the
+translated index. The TLB is already flushed when the boot mode bit
+changes. The microSPARC, TurboSPARC and LEON models are unchanged, and the
+feature can be turned off (-cpu TI-SuperSparc-II,-boot-mode-ifetch).
+OpenBIOS clears boot mode when it enables the MMU (it writes 1 to the
+control register), so it is not affected.
+
+With this the 600MP PROM gets past that test and on to its other CPU
+module tests; it doesn't boot yet (QEMU models none of the SuperSPARC's
+diagnostic store buffer, cache tag or cache data registers, which those
+tests read back).
